==English==
{{was wotd|2008|July|6}}

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|elliptic|al}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|ἐλλειπτικός}}, from {{m|grc|ἐλλείπω||I leave out, omit}}. Surface analysis {{suffix|en|ellipse|ical}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈlɪp.tɪk.əl/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈlɪp.tɪ.kəl/|/əˈlɪp.tɪ.kəl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-elliptical.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# In a shape of, or reminding of, an [[ellipse]]; [[oval]].
#* '''1876''', Edward Roth (translator), ''[[w:All Around the Moon|All Around the Moon]]'', [[s:Around the Moon/Chapter XIX|Chapter XIX]],
#*: Having admitted that the projectile was describing an orbit around the moon, this orbit must necessarily be '''elliptical'''; science proves that it must be so.
# Of, or showing [[ellipsis]]; having a word or words omitted.
#: ''If he is sometimes '''elliptical''' and obscure, it is because he has so much to tell us.'' -- [[w:Edmund Wilson|Edmund Wilson]]
# {{lb|en|of speech}} [[concise|Concise]], [[condensed]].
#* '''1903''', [[w:Gilbert Keith Chesterton|Gilbert Keith Chesterton]], ''Robert Browning'', [[s:Robert Browning/Chapter VI|Chapter VI]],
#*: Browning's dark and '''elliptical''' mode of speech, like his love of the grotesque, was simply a characteristic of his, a trick of his temperament, and had little or nothing to do with whether what he was expressing was profound or superficial.
#* '''early XX c.''', [[s:The Making of a New Yorker|The Making of a New Yorker]], by O. Henry
#*: He was called a [[tramp]]; but that was only an '''elliptical''' way of saying that he was a philosopher, an artist, a traveller, a naturalist and a discoverer.
# {{lb|en|mathematics|rare}} [[elliptic|Elliptic]].

====Usage notes====
* In botanical usage, ''elliptic(al)'' refers only to the general shape of the object (usually a [[leaf]]), independently of its [[apex]] or [[margin]] (and sometimes the [[base]]), so that an "elliptic leaf" may very well be [[pointed]] at both ends. A three-dimensional elliptical object is {{m|en|ellipsoid}}, while an object that is not a perfectly stretched circle is {{m|en|ovoid}} or {{m|en|obovoid}}.

